-- title:
--   $\ker\varphi$ has exactly six elements
-- statement:
--   The kernel of the Georgi-Glashow map is finite of order six:
--
--   $$\bigl|\ker\varphi\bigr| \;=\; 6 .$$
--
--   Combined with the elementwise description of the kernel, this is the assertion $\ker\varphi\cong\mathbb{Z}_6$ of the source: the six elements are $(\alpha, \alpha^{-3}I, \alpha^{2}I)$ as $\alpha$ ranges over the sixth roots of unity, and distinct roots give distinct elements, so the kernel is cyclic of order six generated by the element with $\alpha = e^{2\pi i/6}$.
--
--   The order six is what the Standard Model must respect for the $\mathrm{SU}(5)$ theory to be viable: the group actually acting on the observed fermions is $G_{\mathrm{SM}}/\mathbb{Z}_6$, not $G_{\mathrm{SM}}$.
-- source:
--   John Baez and John Huerta, The Algebra of Grand Unified Theories, https://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/guts.pdf, Section 3.1, p. 34 ('and this is Z₆, because scalar matrices α⁻³ and α² live in SU(2) and SU(3), respectively, if and only if α is a sixth root of unity')

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GUT_standard_model_group

namespace GrandUnifiedTheories

theorem phi_ker_card : Nat.card {x : GSM // phiMatrix x = 1} = 6 := by sorry

end GrandUnifiedTheories
